course: dictionary
lesson: 14719
title: Profane: rude words and not holy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Profane is a formal word, so you will meet it in serious writing and in school rules. Its most common meaning is rude or offensive: profane language is swearing. The clues help you: «The teacher said no profane language is allowed in class» tells you that swear words are not allowed.
explain: Profane can also mean ordinary and not holy. Sacred means holy and special, and profane is its opposite. «The priest blessed the sacred church, not the profane market outside.» The two words sit side by side because they are opposites.
explain: Profane can also be a verb. To profane something holy is to treat it with disrespect. «The thieves profaned the old temple by smashing its statues.» Ask yourself: is the thing special and holy? If someone treats it badly, profane means to disrespect it.
fact: Profane means rude, offensive or disrespectful, especially about religion. Profane language is swearing.
fact: Profane can also mean not religious or ordinary, the opposite of sacred.
fact: To profane a holy place is to treat it with disrespect.
fact: «Profane» is a formal word, so you will find it more in books, speeches and rules than in everyday talk.
fact: Sacred and profane often appear together: sacred means holy, and profane means ordinary or disrespectful towards holy things.
exercise: «The teacher said no profane language is allowed in the classroom.» What does profane mean here? | Very quiet | Happy and kind | *Rude, swearing words | Holy and special
exercise: In religious lessons, sacred means holy. What is the opposite, meaning ordinary and not holy? | Ancient | Sacred | Solemn | *Profane
exercise: «The visitors profaned the holy shrine by writing rude words on its walls.» What does profaned mean? | *Treated with disrespect | Visited for the first time | Painted in bright colours | Cleaned carefully
test: Which word means holy and special? | *Sacred | Everyday | Rude | Profane
test: Choose the best meaning of profane in «Profane language is not allowed at school.» | Holy | Very old | Quiet and polite | *Rude and offensive
exam: What does profane mean in «The monks kept the sacred and the profane things apart»? | Rude | *Ordinary, not holy | Holy | Hidden
